Why Ipv6 :

		- There arent enough IPv4 address available
		
		- The internet have outgrowned the capability of Ipv4
		
			* VLSM, private IPv4 addresses and NAT have been used to conserve the use of IPv4 				  address space
			
			* Short term solutions

		- IANA announce Some regions of the world have ran out of Ipv4 addresses

			* On 9/24/2015 ARIN declared exhaustion of the Ipv4 address pool

IPv6 :

		- An Ipv6 address is 128 bits
		
		- Every additional bit doubles the number of possible addresses
	
		- Number of Addresses 
		
			* IPv6 : 340,282,366,920,938,463,463,374,607,431,768,211,456
		
				ex: 2001:0DB8:5917:EABD:6562:17EA:C92D:59BD / 64
			
			* IPv4 : 4,294,967,296 

				ex: 192.168.155.0 / 24
		
		- Shortening IPv6 addresses

			* Leading 0s can be removed
		
				ex : 
					2001:0DB8:000A:001B:20A1:0020:0080:34BD
							|
							|
					2001:DB8:A:1B:20A1:80:34BD

			* Consecutive quartets of all 0s can be replace with a double colon (::)
			
				ex :

					2001:0DB8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0080:34BD
						|
						|	
					2001:DB8::80:34BD
			
			* Consexutive quartets of 0s can only be abbreviated once in an IPv6

				ex :

					2001:0000:0000:0000:20A1:0000:0000:34BD
					
					2001::20A1::34BD XXXXX ---> 1. This will not work 
	
					2001::20A1:0:0:34BD ---> 2. this work

					*** 2 works because only one side of the Ip address is abbreviated by ::

				example :

				FULL IPv6 Address			      	     Shortened IPv6 Address
			
			2000:0078:0020:01BF:ED89:0000:0000:0001			2000:78:20:1BF:ED89::1

			34EF:230D:0000:0000:00AF:9400:AC85:0009			34EF:230D::AF:9400:AC85:9
			
			0045:0000:0000:0000:0000:54A8:002D:F8B9			45::54A8:2D:F8B9
		
		- Expanding IPv6 addresses

			* Expanding 0 where needed (All quartets should have 4 hexadecimal characters)
		
				ex :

					FE80::2:0:0:FBE8
		
					FE80::0002:0000:0000:FBE8


				- If a double colon is used, replace it with all-0 quartets. Make sure there are
