		- Finding the IPv6 prefix ( global unicast addresses )
		
			* Companies requesting IPv6 addresses from their ISP will receive a /48 block

			* Normaly IPv6 subnets use a /64 prefix length

			* As a result companies have 16 bits to use in order to make subnets

			* The remaining 64 bits can be used for host

				# 48 bits ---> Network Portion

				# 16 bits ---> Subnet Identifier

				# 64 bits ---> Host Portion

				---> Together they make up the complete IPv6 address ( which is 128 bits ) <---

				Ex :

					2001:0DB8:8B00:0001::1 / 64

					2001:0DB8:8B00:0001 ---> 48 bit 'global routing prefix' assigned by the ISP

					0001 ----> 16 bits 'subnet identifier', used by the enterprise to make various subnets

					0000:0000:0000:0001 ---> 64 bit 'interface identifier', the host portion of the address

			* Prefix means Network portion

				# / 64 means the first octates are the network portion because 16+16+16+16 = 64

				# / 56 means the first octate are the network portion but the prefix end in the middle of the 
				  octate
					
					# select the first half of the octate then fill the rest with zeros

					Ex :

						300D:00F2:0B34:2100:0000:0000:1200:0001/56

						300D:F2:B34:2100::/56 ---> Network Portion , everything else will be zeros

				# / 93 mean the first octate are the networking portion

					* First 5 hextact are equal to 80 bits

					* So we split the 6 octate until we reach the correct ammount we need
	
					* then we convert the rest to binary in order to get the value thats located at that 										  place

					ex :

						2001:0DB8:8B00:0001:FB89:017B:0020:0011/93

						2001:0DB8:8B00:0001:FB89 ---> First 5 hextate (80 bit)
					
						017B ---> this whole hextact will make it 96 bits in total but we only need 93 so 											  we split it

							ex :
							       0 1 7 B --> this will need to be converted to binary in order to get the value
							       ^ ^ ^ 																			       | | | 

							       4+4+4 = 12 + 80 --> 92 

							       OxB = 11 
								
							       11 = 1011 in binary , we take one from this to complete the amount of bits we need ( 92 + 1=93)

							       1101
							       ||||		
							       8401 -> we only take the 8
					
						As a result the prefix is 2001:DB8:8B00:1:FB89:178::/93

				